<!-- Question 12 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How does line \(j\), representing \(f(2x)\), compare to line \(m\), representing \(m(x) = 2x\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: The same points are in both functions. The lines appear to be exactly the same.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<!-- Question 13 -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>How does line \(k\), representing \(f(\frac{1}{2} x)\), compare to line \(n\), representing \(n(x) = \frac{1}{2} x\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: The same points are in both functions. The lines appear to be exactly the same.</para>
  </solution></exercise>

<para><term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">Horizontal dilations</term> that stretch and compress <emphasis>linear</emphasis> functions are difficult to identify from graphs because they can appear as if they are vertical dilations. However, they are different because: </para>
<list>
  <item>The changes to the function are applied to the input values of a function when the transformation is a <emphasis>horizontal dilation</emphasis>. </item>
  <item>The changes to the function are applied to the output values of a function when the transformation is a <emphasis>vertical dilation</emphasis>.</item>
</list>
